  "worked_solution": "Let $u={x}^{2}-1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,x\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{3/2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
